# Flutter Component Revision Page — `Stack`

## 1. What is `Stack`?

`Stack` is a layout widget that places multiple widgets **on top of each other**.

### Simple meaning

> `Stack` = Layer widgets over one another.

Unlike:

```text
Row    → side by side
Column → one below another
Stack  → one on top of another
```

---

# 2. Basic Syntax

```dart
Stack(
  children: [
    Widget1(),
    Widget2(),
    Widget3(),
  ],
)
```

`Stack` uses `children` because it can contain multiple widgets.

---

# 3. Simple Example

```dart
Stack(
  children: [
    Container(
      width: 300,
      height: 200,
      color: Colors.blue,
    ),

    Text("Hello"),
  ],
)
```

The `Text` is placed **over the Container**.

Conceptually:

```text
┌──────────────────────┐
│                      │
│       Hello          │
│                      │
└──────────────────────┘
```

---

# 4. Order of Widgets Matters

The widgets are layered according to their order.

```dart
Stack(
  children: [
    Container(color: Colors.blue),
    Container(color: Colors.red),
  ],
)
```

The second widget is drawn **on top of** the first.

Think:

```text
Widget 1 → bottom layer
Widget 2 → middle layer
Widget 3 → top layer
```

So the **last children are generally visually above earlier children**.

---

# 5. Real-World Example — Profile Picture

A common use of `Stack` is a profile picture with an online indicator.

```dart
Stack(
  children: [
    CircleAvatar(
      radius: 40,
      backgroundImage: NetworkImage(
        "https://example.com/profile.jpg",
      ),
    ),

    Positioned(
      right: 0,
      bottom: 0,
      child: Container(
        width: 18,
        height: 18,
        decoration: BoxDecoration(
          color: Colors.green,
          shape: BoxShape.circle,
        ),
      ),
    ),
  ],
)
```

Conceptually:

```text
       🟢
    ┌───────┐
    │       │
    │  👤   │
    │       │
    └───────┘
```

The green circle is placed over the profile picture.

---

# 6. `Positioned`

`Positioned` is commonly used **inside `Stack`**.

It lets you control exactly where a child is placed.

Example:

```dart
Stack(
  children: [
    Container(
      width: 300,
      height: 200,
      color: Colors.blue,
    ),

    Positioned(
      top: 10,
      right: 10,
      child: Icon(Icons.favorite),
    ),
  ],
)
```

The icon is positioned:

```text
10 pixels from the top
10 pixels from the right
```

---

# 7. Important `Positioned` Properties

```dart
Positioned(
  top: 10,
  bottom: 10,
  left: 10,
  right: 10,
  child: Widget(),
)
```

### `top`

Distance from the top.

### `bottom`

Distance from the bottom.

### `left`

Distance from the left.

### `right`

Distance from the right.

You don't have to use all four.

---

# 8. Example — Notification Badge

Imagine a notification icon:

```text
     ③
    🔔
```

You can create it with:

```dart
Stack(
  children: [
    Icon(
      Icons.notifications,
      size: 32,
    ),

    Positioned(
      right: 0,
      top: 0,
      child: Container(
        padding: EdgeInsets.all(4),
        decoration: BoxDecoration(
          color: Colors.red,
          shape: BoxShape.circle,
        ),
        child: Text(
          "3",
          style: TextStyle(
            color: Colors.white,
            fontSize: 10,
          ),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  ],
)
```

This pattern is extremely common in real applications.

---

# 9. `Stack` with Image + Text

You can put text over an image.

```dart
Stack(
  children: [
    Image.network(
      "https://example.com/image.jpg",
      width: 300,
      height: 200,
      fit: BoxFit.cover,
    ),

    Positioned(
      bottom: 10,
      left: 10,
      child: Text(
        "Beautiful Place",
        style: TextStyle(
          color: Colors.white,
          fontSize: 20,
          fontWeight: FontWeight.bold,
        ),
      ),
    ),
  ],
)
```

Result:

```text
┌─────────────────────────┐
│                         │
│        IMAGE            │
│                         │
│ Beautiful Place         │
└─────────────────────────┘
```

---

# 10. `Stack` Without `Positioned`

You don't always need `Positioned`.

```dart
Stack(
  alignment: Alignment.center,
  children: [
    Container(
      width: 200,
      height: 200,
      color: Colors.blue,
    ),

    Icon(
      Icons.play_arrow,
      size: 60,
      color: Colors.white,
    ),
  ],
)
```

The icon appears in the center of the Container.

---

# 11. `alignment`

`Stack` has an `alignment` property.

```dart
Stack(
  alignment: Alignment.center,
  children: [
    Container(
      width: 200,
      height: 200,
      color: Colors.blue,
    ),

    Text("Hello"),
  ],
)
```

The child widgets are aligned according to the specified alignment.

Common values:

```dart
Alignment.center
Alignment.topCenter
Alignment.bottomCenter
Alignment.centerLeft
Alignment.centerRight
Alignment.topLeft
Alignment.topRight
Alignment.bottomLeft
Alignment.bottomRight
```

---

# 12. Real-World Uses

`Stack` is commonly used for:

* Profile picture + online status
* Notification badges
* Text over images
* Play button over video thumbnail
* Favorite icon over product image
* Discount badge
* Loading overlays
* Floating elements
* Image editing interfaces
* Maps with markers
* Custom UI decorations

---

# 13. Stack vs Row vs Column

| Widget   | Arrangement         |
| -------- | ------------------- |
| `Row`    | Horizontal          |
| `Column` | Vertical            |
| `Stack`  | Overlapping/layered |

Think:

```text
Row
A   B   C
```

```text
Column
A
B
C
```

```text
Stack
┌─────────┐
│   B     │
│  ┌───┐  │
│  │ A │  │
│  └───┘  │
└─────────┘
```

---

# 14. Important Rule

`Positioned` should generally be used as a **direct child of `Stack`**.

Correct:

```dart
Stack(
  children: [
    Positioned(
      top: 10,
      right: 10,
      child: Text("Hello"),
    ),
  ],
)
```

You don't normally use:

```dart
Column(
  children: [
    Positioned(...),
  ],
)
```

because `Positioned` is specifically designed to work with `Stack`.

---

# 15. Common Mistake

Don't use `Stack` when you simply want widgets side by side.

Use:

```dart
Row()
```

Don't use `Stack` when you simply want widgets vertically.

Use:

```dart
Column()
```

Use `Stack` when widgets actually need to **overlap or be layered**.

---

# 16. Mental Model

Imagine transparent sheets placed on top of each other:

```text
Layer 3 → Text
Layer 2 → Icon
Layer 1 → Image
```

`Stack` manages those layers.

---

# 17. Quick Revision

```dart
Stack(
  children: [
    BackgroundWidget(),

    Positioned(
      top: 10,
      right: 10,
      child: ForegroundWidget(),
    ),
  ],
)
```

### Remember

> **`Row` → side by side**

> **`Column` → one below another**

> **`Stack` → on top of each other**

> **`Positioned` → control the exact position of a Stack child**

### One-line definition

> **`Stack` is a Flutter layout widget that places multiple widgets on top of each other in layers.**
